VirtualObject::save writes every VObject packed, whichever layout it was
loaded in, and the packed layout cannot hold the fields it drops.

ZenGin stores a VObject in one of two layouts: packed, with every scalar field
in a single `dataRaw` blob, or unpacked, one archive entry per field. `load`
reads both; `save` writes the packed one unconditionally, because the choice
lives in a file-static `pack` that is initialised to `true` and that only
`enable_packed_save` writes.

That is a conversion, not a re-save, and it is lossy in both directions. All
1277 VObjects of a retail Gothic II ASCII world are `pack=int:0` in the
original and `pack=int:1` in ZenKit's re-save, and 43.9% of the ASCII entries
go with them. (It is also the reason the unpacked writer's own defects could
sit unnoticed - see 0045 and 0046, which this patch requires: it is what makes
that writer reachable at all.)

The layout is a property of the file, so `load` now records it on the object
and `save` reproduces it. `enable_packed_save(false)` still forces the unpacked
form for every VObject, which is the only behaviour that switch ever had; the
new member defaults to `true`, so a VObject that was built rather than loaded
is still written packed, as ZenGin's own tools write it. Retail BinSafe worlds
are packed throughout and their bytes are unchanged.

Upstreamable with work: it adds a public field (`packed_layout`), the same
shape and the same justification as 0016's `packed_reserved_bit`, and it
changes what `save` writes for any consumer that loads an unpacked world.

--- a/include/zenkit/vobs/VirtualObject.hh
+++ b/include/zenkit/vobs/VirtualObject.hh
@@ -444,6 +444,14 @@
 		/// \note This property is only available in Gothic II.
 		bool packed_reserved_bit = false;
 
+		/// rief Whether this VObject was stored in the packed layout (`pack=1`).
+		///
+		/// <p>ZenGin writes a VObject either packed — every scalar field in one `dataRaw` blob — or unpacked, one
+		/// archive entry per field; #load reads both. The choice is a property of the file, not of the object, so it
+		/// is kept here and #save reproduces it instead of packing unconditionally. A VObject that was never loaded
+		/// defaults to the packed form, which is what ZenGin's own tools write.</p>
+		bool packed_layout = true;
+
 		/// \brief Indicates the strength of the animation set through #anim_mode.
 		///
 		/// <p>Essentialy, this controls the strength of the wind to be animated using the given #anim_mode. This value
--- a/src/vobs/VirtualObject.cc
+++ b/src/vobs/VirtualObject.cc
@@ -106,6 +106,7 @@
 		Object::load(r, version);
 
 		auto packed = r.read_int() != 0; // pack
+		this->packed_layout = packed;
 		bool has_visual_object = true;
 		bool has_ai_object = true;
 		bool has_event_manager_object = false;
@@ -244,7 +245,12 @@
 	void VirtualObject::save(WriteArchive& w, GameVersion version) const {
 		Object::save(w, version);
 
